  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">DRA Sudan Crisis 2023 SCNL</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="2">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">SC&amp;RAISE will provide MPC and Shelter/NFI support to address the urgent needs of most affected 521 HHs (2606 ind.) in Medani and Kamlin (Al Jazeera) and Damazine (Blue Nile) in ratio 70% - 30%.
Shelter/NFIs: 216 emergency NFI kits will be distributed to address the urgent need of 1080 people in need in both locations. The kits will include bedding material, jerry-cans for water storage, utensils, mosquito nets and cleaning tools in line with the findings of RNA and guidelines from the Shelter/NFI Cluster (2023). The distribution of NFI kits aims to improve living conditions, reduce risks associated with harsh weather and disease outbreaks, and restore the dignity of affected households. 
Cash for Rent: SC will offer 3 rounds of cash for rent (700$ in total) to 46HHs (230 individuals) including single female-headed HHs with children or PwD to support them in moving from unsafe gathering sites to more appropriate emergency shelter solutions, ensuring their safety and well-being. 
MPC: SC will provide 3 rounds of MPC (each 17$) to 1296 individuals to help alleviate the financial burden on displaced families and cover the cost for ex. food and hygiene items. SC is currently implementing an MPC project, and assessments show that most markets are stable enough to provide cash support.  The chosen modality is the Mobile Transfer or Hawala, and cash agents for cash distribution where appropriate. 
SC&amp;RAISE will ensure to do no harm and maximize the outcome of the project by mainstreaming protection partners&#x2019; staffs being trained on on child protection and safeguarding policies and procedures, risk assessment, management, and reporting mechanisms.  SC&amp;RAISE will disseminate messages related to child protection to targeted families and caregivers and will assist the most vulnerable or at-risk families in obtaining cash grants and incorporating into the social protection system. The intervention emphasizes gender sensitivity, with a focus on promoting gender equality.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="3">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">SC&amp;RAISE will target newly displaced residing in camps or gathering sites, including public buildings, open spaces or renting accommodation. The target group will include flood-affected communities and new returnees in areas exposed to short-term clashes where safety has been restored. Beneficiary selection will follow Shelter Cluster recommendations. The criteria to select the targeted HHs are: Female/ Child/ Elderly headed HHs; IPDs, HHs with multiple children or +1 children with a disability; PLWs; separated or unaccompanied children; PwD or chronic illness people; Youth.

For the cash for rent component, targeting will be defined further after conducting a more detailed assessment. SC will consult with children and their families throughout the selection process of beneficiaries and NFIs and methods of receiving assistance. All SC staffs are being trained on Psychological First Aid, enabling an appropriate and safe response to individuals and groups experiencing (severe) distress.</narrative>
  </description>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The conflict in Sudan has resulted in widespread violence, displacement, and urgent humanitarian needs. The Sudanese Armed Forces (SAF) and Rapid Support Forces (RSF) continue to engage in fighting despite a ceasefire. As of 23 May, the UNOCHA reported at least 730 deaths and 5.500 injuries while around 1.4M people have been displaced since 15 April (74% being IDPs and 26% left the country). 
According to HAC Commissioner, Al Jazira has received around 390,000 IDPs since the conflict started, with numbers growing dramatically. As stated in the RNA conducted by SC in May 2023, the congested living conditions and lack of privacy in gathering sites pose risks of gender-based violence and there is urgent need for food, health, hygiene, and NFIs i.e. mattresses, jerry-cans, utensils, and mosquito nets.
Given the increasing influx of IDPs from Khartoum into Blue Nile, and pre-existing displacements from inter-communal conflicts between tribes, shelter and NFI assistance is urgently needed to withstand the rainy season (DTM-IOM). Furthermore, markets are impacted by the conflict, with dramatic price increases for staple food (+50%) and goods and shortages of imported products such as medicine and consumables (RNA).</narrative>
  </description>
